+NOTES = r"""
+Paper boundary. Section 1 fixes the definitions used here.  An ordering is
+valid when its displayed partial sums are pairwise distinct; it is a sequencing
+when, additionally, every partial sum except possibly the final one is nonzero.
+Theorems 1.3 and 1.4 are sparse-regime existence statements.  Their proof uses
+the random block construction of Section 2 and the Lovasz Local Lemma in Lemma
+2.7.  It proves positive probability, but does not output a certificate by a
+polynomial-time algorithm.  The paper also records the easy/proved regimes:
+sets of size at most 12, Kravitz's log(p)/log log(p) range, Theorem 1.2's
+exp(c(log p)^(1/4)) range, and Theorem 1.3's exp(c(log p)^(1/3)) range.  The dense
+instances here deliberately do not turn those existence bounds into a false
+Track-A distributional-hardness claim.
+
+Generation.  For prime k, choose a primitive root q and a nonzero scale u, and
+plant a_i=u(q-1)q^i for 0<=i<n<k-1.  Its j-th partial sum is exactly
+u(q^(j+1)-1), so primitivity makes all partial sums nonzero and distinct.  This
+is inverse generation plus composition of the geometric-series identity: no
+sequencing is searched for.  A random scale and shuffled storage remove numeric
+position and magnitude signatures.
+
+Track B and attacks.  The successful reference algorithm counts all pairwise
+quotients in A, finds the ratio occurring n-1 times, and walks the resulting
+cycle; it is disclosed in G6.  The compact route notices that A omits only
+k-1-n residues: those omitted residues form the much shorter complementary
+geometric run.  The failing panel checks increasing-residue/outlier order,
+smallest-feasible greedy construction, 256 randomized feasible-prefix restarts,
+and alternating low/high residues.  canonical_key quotients out all cyclic-group
+automorphisms x -> ux and all reorderings of the input set.
+""".strip()
